# Day 27

**30km / 8.5 hours**

This was a long day in terms of distance but not a tricky day. It began in the early morning as we were conscious that it would be best to cross the estuary as close to low tide as possible and the low tide was around 7:40am. 

Even when crossing at low tide the water was still up to just under my waist which emphasises how impossible it would be at high tide. I could write some prose here about the icy cool water in the morning and how it chilled my spirits. I might go on by mentioning the apprehension with each step as it's hard to tell how deep it will get each step. However I've already skipped to the punchline where I cross without major issues, just some wet legs which dry off well enough. 

![A nice beach in the morning cloud](./beach.jpg)

Continuing onwards was a pleasant coastal walk until getting to a carpark after which was just a cycle of beach and suburban sidewalks. The beaches were all fantastic but typical beaches and the sidewalks was passing these massive houses. Typical Auckland. 

My prevailing thought is that Auckland is wasted on Aucklanders. The greatest thing about the city is the natural beauty and almost everything built on it only serves to sully that. There was the awesome Rangitoto island off to the left most of the day, the amazing beaches, the extraordinary rocks and cliffs, the volcanic mountains and soil. And yet what do I see walking through it? Soulless houses and people without purpose, just coasting through on vanity and self aggrandisement.

I left the previous paragraph there because it reflects how I feel but I acknowledge that logically it's not true. Aucklanders are people just like everyone else and I do fundamentally believe that most people are good. It remains as a testament to the vindictive, selfish, arrogant aspect of myself of which I would like to excise.

![The beautiful Rangitoto with a nice beach in the foreground](./rangitoto.jpg)

Anyway the walk was nice enough. It capped off with a ferry ride into the centre of the city, the belly of the beast. In Auckland I feel a bit like Homer Simpson in New York, just expecting things to go wrong. It is convenient having so many shops in close proximity though.